// ==WindhawkMod==
// @id              hyprland-windows
// @name            Hyprland Windows
// @description     Hyprland-style window handling: move, resize, maximize and close windows with Win + mouse from anywhere on them, hide title bars, and color window borders
// @version         1.0.1
// @author          hiword9
// @github          https://github.com/HiWord9
// @include         *
// @compilerOptions -lcomctl32 -ldwmapi
// @license         GPL-3.0
// ==/WindhawkMod==

// ==WindhawkModReadme==
/*
# Hyprland Windows

Window handling the way Hyprland does it, on Windows.

* **Win + left mouse button** - drag a window from anywhere on it, not just by
  its title bar.
* **Win + right mouse button** - resize a window from anywhere on it, from
  whichever corner is closest to the cursor.
* **Win + double click** - maximize a window, or restore it. The title bar is
  gone, so the double click that used to be on it now works anywhere on the
  window; the settings offer other things for it to do.
* **Win + middle click** - close the window under the cursor. The button, the
  modifiers and the action are all settings; it can be a key instead, such as
  Win+Q - even one Windows already uses, like Win+W.
* **Ctrl+Alt+H** - hide the focused window's title bar; press it again to bring
  the title bar back.

Moving and resizing feel like the real thing: windows still snap to the screen
edges, dragging one to the top still offers the Windows 11 snap layouts, and
`Esc` cancels a drag in progress. A window you are dragging fades to slightly
translucent while you hold it, the way a Hyprland window does. Hold `Ctrl` as
well and its edges turn magnetic: bring one close to another window or to the
edge of the screen and it lines up with it. Hold `Shift` while resizing to
keep the window's proportions.

Title bars can also go away on their own: with hiding by default turned on,
every new window opens without one, except the programs on a list of
exceptions (Paint and the Snipping Tool to begin with).

Give windows a border color - your own, or `accent` to follow the Windows
accent color - and it fades from one color to the other as focus moves, the
way a Hyprland border does. That goes for every window with a frame, or only
for the ones whose title bar is hidden, whichever the settings say.

Everything is configurable in the settings - the hotkey, the modifier key, the
translucency, the magnet, the border, and how much of the frame goes away with
the title bar.

## Good to know

* Apps that draw their own title bar instead of using the system one (Chrome,
  Electron apps, VS Code, Office) are not affected by the hotkey.
* A window with a classic menu bar (File, Edit, ...) can't keep that menu where it
  is once the title bar is gone, so by default it is hidden along with it and
  stays reachable from the keyboard with `Alt` or `F10`. The settings offer the
  other choices.
* `Win` + mouse doesn't work over content drawn with WinUI or XAML - Paint,
  Windows Terminal, the tabs and address bar of File Explorer: Windows hands
  those clicks to the app past the point where the mod sees them.
*/
// ==/WindhawkModReadme==

// ==WindhawkModSettings==
/*
- hotkey: Ctrl+Alt+H
  $name: Title bar hotkey
  $description: >-
    Hides or restores the title bar of the focused window. Combine Ctrl, Alt,
    Shift and Win with "+", for example Ctrl+Alt+H or Win+Shift+T. Set it to
    "none" to turn the hotkey off. It takes precedence over a Windows shortcut
    on the same keys.
- dragModifier: win
  $name: Move/resize modifier key
  $description: >-
    Hold this key and drag with the left mouse button to move a window, or with
    the right mouse button to resize it
  $options:
  - win: Win (Super)
  - alt: Alt
- dragTranslucency: fade
  $name: Translucency while dragging
  $description: >-
    Fade a window to translucent while it is being dragged, the way Hyprland
    does, and back when the drag ends
  $options:
  - fade: While moving and while resizing
  - move: While moving only
  - opaque: Never
- dragOpacity: 85
  $name: Opacity while dragging
  $description: >-
    How opaque the window stays during a drag, in percent. 100 is fully
    opaque, 10 is barely there.
- dragFadeIn: 120
  $name: Fade-in time
  $description: How long the window takes to get there, in milliseconds
- dragFadeOut: 60
  $name: Fade-out time
  $description: >-
    How long it takes to come back once the drag ends, in milliseconds
- snap: both
  $name: Magnetic edges while dragging
  $description: >-
    A window being moved or resized sticks to the edges of other windows and
    of the screen when it comes close, the way a floating window does in
    Hyprland.
  $options:
  - both: To other windows and to the screen
  - windows: To other windows only
  - monitor: To the screen only
  - none: Off
- snapDistance: 12
  $name: Magnet strength
  $description: >-
    How close an edge has to come before it sticks, in pixels at 100% scaling
- snapWindowGap: 0
  $name: Gap between windows
  $description: >-
    How much room to leave when a window sticks to another one, in pixels.
    0 puts them edge to edge.
- snapMonitorGap: 0
  $name: Gap at the screen edge
  $description: >-
    How much room to leave when a window sticks to the edge of the screen,
    in pixels
- snapModifier: ctrl
  $name: Extra key for the magnet
  $description: >-
    A key held along with the move/resize modifier, so that edges are only
    magnetic when you want them to be. "none" makes them always magnetic.
  $options:
  - ctrl: Ctrl
  - shift: Shift
  - alt: Alt
  - none: No extra key
- snapModifierWhen: hold
  $name: What that key does
  $options:
  - hold: The edges are magnetic only while it is held
  - suppress: The edges are magnetic unless it is held
- keepAspectModifier: shift
  $name: Keep the shape while resizing
  $description: >-
    Hold this key while resizing a window to keep its proportions. The magnet
    steps aside while it is held.
  $options:
  - shift: Shift
  - ctrl: Ctrl
  - alt: Alt
  - none: Off
- doubleClickAction: maximize
  $name: Modifier + double click
  $description: >-
    What a double click with the modifier above held does to the window under
    the cursor. Worth having because a window with no title bar has nowhere
    left to double click.
  $options:
  - maximize: Maximize the window, or restore it if it is maximized
  - titleBar: Hide the window's title bar, or bring it back
  - close: Close the window
  - none: Nothing
- doubleClickTime: 0
  $name: Double click speed
  $description: >-
    How long the second click may take, in milliseconds. 0 follows the
    double-click speed from the Windows mouse settings.
- windowShortcut: Win+MButton
  $name: Window shortcut
  $description: >-
    A key or a mouse button that acts on a window: with a mouse button it is
    the window under the cursor, with a key the focused one. Combine Ctrl,
    Alt, Shift and Win with "+", and use MButton, XButton1 or XButton2 for
    the mouse - for example Win+MButton, Alt+XButton1 or Win+Q. A key takes
    precedence over a Windows shortcut on the same keys, so Win+W can close
    windows instead of opening the widgets. Set it to "none" to turn it off.
    Left and right are taken by move and resize, and binding one of them here
    takes it away from the drag.
- windowShortcutAction: close
  $name: What the window shortcut does
  $options:
  - close: Close the window
  - maximize: Maximize the window, or restore it if it is maximized
  - titleBar: Hide the window's title bar, or bring it back
  - none: Nothing
- topEdgeResize: false
  $name: Resize from the top edge
  $description: >-
    When the title bar is hidden, the top few pixels of the window act as a
    resize handle, like the other edges. Off by default, because it takes those
    pixels away from whatever the app draws there.
- menuBarWindows: hide
  $name: Windows with a menu bar
  $description: >-
    What to do with windows that have a classic menu bar below the title bar
  $options:
  - hide: Hide the menu bar too (keyboard access via Alt / F10 still works)
  - keepMenu: Keep the menu bar, remove only the title bar
  - skip: Don't touch such windows
- hideByDefault: false
  $name: Hide title bars by default
  $description: >-
    Automatically hide the title bar of every new window. Use the hotkey to
    bring it back for a specific window.
- hideByDefaultExclude:
  - mspaint.exe
  - SnippingTool.exe
  $name: Keep the title bar in these programs
  $description: >-
    Programs "Hide title bars by default" leaves alone, by file name (for
    example notepad.exe). The hotkey still hides their title bar. Paint and
    the Snipping Tool are here because Win + mouse can't move them, so without
    a title bar there is nothing left to grab them by.
- borderActive: ""
  $name: Border color of the active window
  $description: >-
    "#RRGGBB" for a color, "accent" for the Windows accent color, "none" for
    no border, empty for the system default.
- borderInactive: ""
  $name: Border color of inactive windows
  $description: >-
    "#RRGGBB" for a color, "accent" for the Windows accent color, "none" for
    no border, empty for the system default.
- borderFramelessOnly: false
  $name: Only color windows with a hidden title bar
  $description: >-
    Off, the two colors above apply to every window with a frame. On, only to
    the ones the mod has taken the title bar from.
- borderTransition: fade
  $name: Border color transition
  $description: >-
    What happens to the border color when a window gains or loses focus. Only
    has anything to fade when both colors above are set to a color - "none"
    and the system default are not colors to fade through.
  $options:
  - fade: Fade from one color to the other
  - instant: Change at once
- borderFadeDuration: 150
  $name: Border fade time
  $description: How long that fade takes, in milliseconds
- corners: default
  $name: Corners of windows with a hidden title bar
  $options:
  - default: System default
  - round: Rounded
  - roundsmall: Slightly rounded
  - none: Square
*/
// ==/WindhawkModSettings==
